---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Photography Perspective Lab: Explore Distance & Focal Length"
  description="Learn how camera position changes perspective. Explore distance, focal length and landscape composition in a practical photography learning lab."
>
  <section class="intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">A different point of view</p>
    <h1>
      Photography
      <br />
      Perspective Lab
    </h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The most powerful change to a photograph can be where you stand. Explore
      the relationship between camera position, framing and depth.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="feature" aria-labelledby="simulator-heading">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">The perspective simulator</p>
      <h2 id="simulator-heading">
        One scene.
        <br />
        Different viewpoints.
      </h2>
      <p>
        Camera position controls perspective. Focal length controls field of
        view. Start with that distinction.
      </p>
      <a class="button" href="/perspective-simulator/">
        Explore the simulator <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <p class="small">Change your viewpoint and save two views to compare.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="depth-study-image">
      <img
        src="/images/depth-study-clean.webp"
        width="560"
        height="280"
        alt="The same-sized hiker appears large nearby, smaller in the middle distance and smallest far away beside a waterfall. Same size in the scene. Different size in the frame."
        decoding="async"
      />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="learning">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2>Build your understanding</h2>
      <strong class="small">From observation to intention</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="learning-intro">
      <p>
        I created this site to help you understand how perspective works in
        photography. I'll show you how moving your position, or your camera height,
        affects the size, spacing, and relationship between your subjects and the
        backgrounds, and why zooming with a telephoto lens produces a different result
        than just moving your camera.
      </p>
      <p>
        Try the interactive simulator, explore the lessons, and test yourself with
        a few challenges and a quiz. My goal is to help you see perspective
        differently and use it to create stronger photographs.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="cards">
      <a class="card" href="/how-perspective-works/">
        <span class="eyebrow">01 / The essentials</span>
        <h3>How perspective works</h3>
        <p>
          Nearby objects look larger because they're closer to your camera.
          Learn how moving forwards, sideways or changing height affects their
          size and overlap against the background. Use these changes to choose
          what stands out in your photograph.
        </p>
        <span>Read the introduction ↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/camera-distance-perspective/">
        <span class="eyebrow">02 / Your position</span>
        <h3>Move first. Look again.</h3>
        <p>
          Move closer to give a foreground rock more presence. Step back to make
          the mountain behind it look larger in comparison. Learn how to balance
          near and far subjects, then compare viewpoints while keeping the
          foreground similarly framed.
        </p>
        <span>Explore camera distance ↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/focal-length-vs-perspective/">
        <span class="eyebrow">03 / Your framing</span>
        <h3>Separate lens from location</h3>
        <p>
          Zooming changes how much of the scene you include. Moving changes how
          objects relate to each other. Learn why a longer lens alone doesn't
          compress a landscape, and when to adjust your focal length or choose a
          different viewpoint.
        </p>
        <span>Explore focal length ↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="bottom-links">
    <a href="/landscape-perspective/">Apply it to landscapes</a>
    <a href="/perspective-challenges/">Perspective challenges</a>
    <a href="/perspective-quiz/">Check your understanding</a>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
